$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>An order on paper, nothing in hand</h2>
<p>The petitioners — a wife and her minor daughter — had done everything the system asks of a maintenance claimant. They obtained an interim maintenance order of Rs. 4,000 per month each in 2023, effective from 26 November 2019, together with litigation expenses. The order attained finality. They then filed execution proceedings. What defeated them was not law but geography: the husband had disappeared, and when they could not supply his current address, the Family Court dismissed the execution for non-prosecution on 5 February 2026. The family was left holding a final order worth years of arrears, with no mechanism to realise a rupee of it.</p>

<h2>Default cannot become defence</h2>
<p>The High Court's reasoning rests on a simple inversion: the dismissal rewarded the very conduct the law seeks to punish. A judgment debtor who evades service and conceals his whereabouts commits a continuing default; to close the execution because of that concealment "would allow the defaulter to convert his own default into a defence." The Court emphasised that maintenance liability is continuing in nature and is discharged only by actual payment — not by the failure of any particular coercive step, and certainly not by the debtor's success in staying hidden. A dismissal with liberty to refile, the Court added, is no substitute: it simply restarts the claimant's ordeal from zero.</p>

<h2>What the executing court must now do</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>The High Court restored the execution to its original number and directed the Family Court to:</p>
<p>— take effective steps to trace and serve the husband, including police assistance from the SHO of the concerned police station, who must file a status report on the steps taken;</p>
<p>— enquire into the judgment debtor's residence, employment and assets, including by summoning records from employers and banks;</p>
<p>— use the statutory enforcement modes available, rather than treating the claimant's inability to supply an address as the end of the matter.</p>
</div>
<p>The order draws on the established position that maintenance orders are enforceable through the full machinery of the law — the provisions formerly found in Sections 125(3) and 128 CrPC (now Sections 144(3) and 147 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023), Section 20(6) of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, and the execution provisions of the Code of Civil Procedure, including Sections 51, 55, 58 and 60 read with Order XXI.</p>

<h2>Why this matters beyond one family</h2>
<p>Execution is where maintenance law most often fails in practice. Orders are won after years of litigation and then die in the execution court because the respondent has changed jobs, moved cities or simply stopped appearing. This decision arms claimants with a clear proposition: the burden of tracing an absconding husband lies on the court's machinery, not on the wife. Practitioners should cite it when an executing court suggests that the matter be "closed with liberty" for want of a current address — and should affirmatively invoke the menu of coercive options it lists, from police tracing and asset disclosure to employer and bank summonses. For respondents, the message is equally plain: disappearance buys time, not immunity, and arrears accumulate with interest in the background.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article summarises a judicial order for general information and is not legal advice. The enforcement steps appropriate in a given execution depend on the terms of the underlying order and the judgment debtor's circumstances.</p>
</div>
